\section{Introduction}

General relativity (GR) describes spacetime dynamics via the Einstein field
equations,
\begin{equation}
    G_{\mu\nu} + \Lambda g_{\mu\nu}
    = \frac{8\pi G}{c^4} T_{\mu\nu},
\end{equation}
where $G_{\mu\nu}$ is the Einstein tensor, $g_{\mu\nu}$ the metric, $\Lambda$
the cosmological constant, and $T_{\mu\nu}$ the stress-energy tensor.
Numerical relativity (NR) solves these equations in $3+1$ form for strong-field
astrophysical scenarios such as binary black hole mergers.

VST-SIM V2.4 extends standard NR frameworks in three key directions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A \emph{vulcanized} spacetime model introducing an elastic hysteresis
    tensor $H_{\mu\alpha\beta}$ that stabilizes curvature evolution and mitigates
    singularity formation.
    \item A \emph{homomorphic} CKKS-based cryptographic pipeline enabling
    encrypted evaluation of metric and curvature tensors.
    \item An \emph{autonomous intelligence loop} coordinating shock capturing,
    homomorphic bootstrapping, and predictive AMR refinement.
\end{itemize}

\section{Theoretical Framework}

\subsection{ADM $3+1$ decomposition}

We adopt the standard $3+1$ ADM foliation of spacetime into spatial hypersurfaces
$\Sigma_t$ with metric $\gamma_{ij}$, lapse $\alpha$, and shift $\beta^i$:
\begin{equation}
    ds^2 = -\alpha^2 dt^2 + \gamma_{ij}
    (dx^i + \beta^i dt)(dx^j + \beta^j dt).
\end{equation}
The extrinsic curvature $K_{ij}$ encodes the embedding of $\Sigma_t$ in the
four-dimensional spacetime.

The evolution equations in ADM form are
\begin{align}
    \partial_t \gamma_{ij}
    &= -2\alpha K_{ij} + \mathcal{L}_\beta \gamma_{ij}, \\
    \partial_t K_{ij}
    &= -D_i D_j \alpha + \alpha \left(
        R^{(3)}_{ij} + K K_{ij} - 2 K_{ik} K^k{}_j
    \right) \nonumber \\
    &\quad + \alpha \frac{8\pi G}{c^4}
    \left( S_{ij} - \frac{1}{2} \gamma_{ij} (S - \rho) \right)
    + \mathcal{L}_\beta K_{ij},
\end{align}
where $R^{(3)}_{ij}$ is the Ricci tensor of $\gamma_{ij}$, $K = \gamma^{ij}K_{ij}$,
$\rho$ is energy density, and $S_{ij}$ the spatial stress tensor.

\subsection{Vulcanized hysteresis tensor}

We introduce a non-linear elastic hysteresis tensor $H_{\mu\alpha\beta}$ that
modifies the Einstein equations:
\begin{equation}
    G_{\mu\nu}
    + H^{\alpha|\beta|\rho}{}_{\mu\nu} R_{\alpha|\beta|\rho}
    + \Lambda g_{\mu\nu}
    = \frac{8\pi G}{c^4} T_{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}
In $3+1$ form, this yields an additional vulcanized force term
$f_{\text{vulcanized}}$ in the $K_{ij}$ evolution:
\begin{align}
    \gamma_{ij}^{\text{next}
    } &= \gamma_{ij} - 2\alpha K_{ij} \Delta t, \\
    K_{ij}^{\text{next}}
    &= K_{ij} + \Delta t \Big[
        R^{(3)}_{ij} + K K_{ij} - 2K_{ik}K^k{}_j
        - D_i D_j \alpha \nonumber \\
        &\quad + \frac{8\pi G}{c^4}
        \left(S_{ij} - \frac{1}{2}\gamma_{ij}(S - \rho)\right)
        + f_{\text{vulcanized}}(\gamma_{ij}, K_{ij}, S_{ij})
    \Big].
\end{align}

The hysteresis tensor is designed such that
\begin{equation}
    \norm{H_{\mu\alpha\beta}} < \norm{K_{ij}},
\end{equation}
ensuring that the additional elastic response remains bounded relative to the
extrinsic curvature flow and does not destabilize the evolution.

